{"profile":{"name":"Vincent Crisanti","ward":"Ward 1, Etobicoke North","bio":"Vincent Crisanti (born 1953) worked as an advertising and signage sales manager before entering municipal politics; he served as an Etobicoke city councillor before amalgamation in 1995. He was elected to Toronto City Council in 2010, re-elected in 2014, served as Deputy Mayor for Etobicoke (2014-2017), lost his seat to Michael Ford in 2018, and returned to council in the October 2022 election, winning Ward 1 Etobicoke North with 41% of the vote. He currently serves on the city's Audit Committee and the Planning and Housing Committee, sits on the Board of Governors of Exhibition Place, and is the City's Seniors Advocate.","committees":["Planning and Housing Committee","Audit Committee","Board of Governors of Exhibition Place"],"contact":{"email":"councillor_crisanti@toronto.ca","phone":"416-397-9255"},"promises":[{"text":"Pledged to improve city infrastructure by encouraging investment in road resurfacing and sidewalk work in Etobicoke North.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-new-city-councillor-elects/"},{"text":"Pledged to push for improvements to the public realm, including upgrades to public parks and community centres.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-new-city-councillor-elects/"},{"text":"Pledged to set an agreement in motion for a light rail extension that would extend the train route from Humber College to a new GO Station at Belfield and Highway 27, consistent with his long-standing advocacy for extending the Finch West LRT to the Etobicoke North GO Station.","source":"https://storeys.com/toronto-new-city-councillor-elects/"}]},"lobbying":{"total":834,"term":259,"first":"2010-12-13","last":"2026-10-08","lobbyists":174,"clients":162,"subjects":372,"methods":{"Meetings":322,"Emails":369,"Phone calls":71,"Written":64,"Other or unreported":8},"top":[{"label":"Economic Development","count":221},{"label":"Licences / Licensing","count":150},{"label":"By-law / Regulation","coun